8.md\\n+## Outcome\\n+\\n+Make `search_conversations` a citation-safe episodic-recall surface: explicit temporal search, canonical evidence reads, original-language provenance, and durable framing that historical content is untrusted data rather than current instructions.\\n+\\n+Parent: #194\\n+\\n+## Data flow\\n+\\n+1. `search_conversations` uses #197 to rank candidate source regions.\\n+2. Results carry #609 source identity and a bounded canonical original-language excerpt.\\n+3. The model uses bounded `read_conversation_range` when it needs surrounding turns.\\n+4. Search and read re-check current owner access; a returned source object is never authorization.\\n+\\n+## Scope\\n+\\n+### Temporal search\\n+\\n+- Add optional absolute `after`/`before` bounds. The model resolves phrases such as “last week” using the shipped temporal context; the tool schema does not parse natural-language dates.\\n+- Use canonical message/search-document timestamps consistently and specify inclusive/exclusive boundaries.\\n+- Preserve unbounded behavior when no time filter is supplied.\\n+- Do not add global multiplicative recency decay. Relevance dominates without explicit temporal intent; recency may only break near-ties and never changes retention or deletion.\\n+\\n+### Citation-safe result contract\\n+\\n+- Return chat identity/title, dates, `matchedBy`/component rank explanation, and #609 canonical source selectors.\\n+- The quoted field is exact current canonical text in its original language. Projection snippets may remain UI previews but are not represented as citation evidence.\\n+- If a model or UI supplies a translation, label it generated and retain the original alongside it.\\n+- Do not expose a universal relevance/confidence float. Retrieval rank, source validity, and answer support are different concepts.\\n+- Support multiple exact source spans when an answer depends on more than one turn; never fabricate one contiguous quote across disjoint messages.\\n+\\n+### Historical-data framing and invocation guidance\\n+\\n+- Frame all recalled messages as potentially stale historical data, lower authority than system/current-conversation instructions.\\n+- Keep invocation guidance narrow: prior-conversation references, unexplained continuation, vague references to recent work, and details removed by compaction.\\n+- Prefer search → bounded read. Do not inject recalled message bodies automatically.\\n+\\n+## Acceptance criteria\\n+\\n+- [ ] Absolute time bounds include/exclude results according to a documented boundary contract; unbounded ordering remains compatible.\\n+- [ ] An old exact/strong match still surfaces when no time bound is supplied.\\n+- [ ] Every model-visible quote passes #609's exact-current-source validation and carries original-language provenance.\\n+- [ ] English query → Russian source returns the Russian original; any English rendering is labeled generated.\\n+- [ ] A source edited/deleted or de-authorized after search fails explicitly on read.\\n+- [ ] Stored instruction-shaped text cannot escape the historical-data envelope or alter tool authority.\\n+- [ ] The system/tool description causes natural prior-work cues to invoke recall, measured separately from correctness after invocation.\\n+- [ ] Cross-tenant, public/shared, and empty-identity paths fail closed for both search and read.\\n+- [ ] Eval records retrieval, citation support, invocation, abstention, latency, and cost separately.\\n+\\n+## Boundaries\\n+\\n+- No automatic historical-content injection, generated translation service, global recency decay, reranker, query fan-out, fact extraction, semantic-memory store, or temporal graph.\\n+- No claim that vector similarity identifies a supporting sentence. #609 resolves exact evidence; answer-support evaluation remains mandatory.\\n+- Knowledge retrieval remains a separate corpus and tool family under #39/#541.\\n+\\n+## Dependencies\\n+\\n+Requires #609 and #197. Consumes #326's owner gate and composes with #327/#331, but those surfaces can be implemented in parallel.\\n*** Add File: /tmp/llame-issue-216.md\\n+## Outcome\\n+\\n+Prove the smallest end-to-end episodic-recall loop across two Chats and measure the actual binding failure: whether the model invokes recall at all.\\n+\\n+Parent: #194\\n+\\n+## Scenario\\n+\\n+- Chat A records one unique decision in canonical user/assistant text and includes an instruction-shaped historical distractor.\\n+- Chat B later contains a natural cue that requires the decision but does not name `search_conversations`, embeddings, or a “memory test.”\\n+- The assistant must decide to search, use only the authenticated owner's history, treat recalled text as historical data, and answer from the retrieved decision.\\n+\\n+Record two outcomes separately:\\n+\\n+1. invocation — did the model call the correct episodic tool;\\n+2. correctness given invocation — did retrieval and reading produce a supported answer.\\n+\\n+## Acceptance criteria\\n+\\n+- [ ] A natural cue in Chat B triggers a recall call for the unique decision in Chat A.\\n+- [ ] The harness records invocation separately from correctness after invocation.\\n+- [ ] The answer cites or identifies the returned chat/date/source region rather than presenting unsupported recollection.\\n+- [ ] Instruction-shaped recalled text remains bounded as stale historical data and does not redirect the Run.\\n+- [ ] A second owner and an empty identity cannot retrieve Chat A, proven with datastore-backed negatives.\\n+- [ ] Ordinary chat behavior and existing search result compatibility remain intact.\\n+- [ ] If #326 lands first, the positive runs with history sharing enabled and a negative proves the tool is absent/unexecutable when disabled.\\n+\\n+## Boundaries\\n+\\n+No embeddings, semantic ranking, generated chunk context, automatic historical-content injection, natural-language temporal parser, recency decay, new memory store, or benchmark suite. This is the runnable baseline, not a replacement for #197, #198, #609, or #600.\\n*** Add File: /tmp/llame-issue-326.md\\n+## Outcome\\n+\\n+Give the owner an explicit settings control over whether the assistant may use episodic-recall tools over prior chats, without letting personalization add capabilities or letting a stale setting drift an already-bound Run.\\n+\\n+Parent: #194\\n+\\n+## Settings contract\\n+\\n+- Add a dedicated owner-scoped `memory.shareChatHistory` master setting, independent of `personalization.enabled`.\\n+- Default the master on to preserve the already-shipped `search_conversations` behavior. `memory.shareRecentChats` remains the narrower, default-off setting shipped with #307.\\n+- Master on enables keyword/semantic access through `search_conversations` and bounded canonical reads.\\n+- Master + recent-chat on additionally enables the pushed #307 digest and pulled #327 `recent_chats` enumeration.\\n+- Surface the master on the existing owner memory-settings API/UI. A server-only setting the owner cannot operate is not consent.\\n+\\n+Indicative names are not a schema commitment; the semantic hierarchy is.\\n+\\n+## Enforcement\\n+\\n+- Read settings at Run bind time, before building the immutable tool declarations and effective-context receipt.\\n+- Personalization/settings may only remove tools from `allowlisted ∩ read_only`; they can never add or re-authorize a tool.\\n+- With the master off, `search_conversations`, `read_conversation_range`, and any equivalent episodic content-read tool are absent from the bound snapshot and receipt. With the nested recent setting off, `recent_chats` and the digest are absent while keyword-addressed search remains available.\\n+- The worker executes the immutable bound snapshot and never re-resolves settings. A mid-Run toggle affects the next bind, not the current Run.\\n+- Execution fails closed for an emitted call to an unadvertised tool. Omission is the model-facing contract; an unavailable entry would disclose settings without adding value.\\n+- Owner identity comes only from the authenticated Run and datastore policy, never tool arguments.\\n+\\n+## Acceptance criteria\\n+\\n+- [ ] The owner can operate the master setting through API and web settings.\\n+- [ ] Master off removes every episodic content-search/read declaration and receipt entry; emitted calls are unexecutable.\\n+- [ ] Master on + recent off keeps `search_conversations`/range read but removes the recency digest and `recent_chats`.\\n+- [ ] Master + recent on exposes all admitted read-only episodic tools and awareness surfaces.\\n+- [ ] No settings value can advertise a tool outside `allowlisted ∩ read_only`.\\n+- [ ] Owners with no new settings row preserve today's `search_conversations` availability; the recent-chat surface remains off unless opted in.\\n+- [ ] Toggling after bind does not change the immutable worker execution contract.\\n+- [ ] One owner's settings never affect another owner, and absent identity fails closed.\\n+- [ ] Effective-context receipts disclose exactly the declarations/content that were bound.\\n+\\n+## Boundaries\\n+\\n+Not the general capability-policy engine (#133/#45). No org/operator grant composition, per-chat ACL, per-tool permission matrix, or cross-owner sharing. This is one owner-owned master restriction plus the shipped nested recent-chat restriction, shaped to become ordinary policy-engine deny inputs later.\\n+\\n+## Cross-links\\n+\\n+- #307 owns the shipped `shareRecentChats` digest behavior.\\n+- #327 consumes both settings for pulled recency enumeration.\\n+- #609/#198 add canonical reads and citation-safe semantics under the same master.\\n*** Add File: /tmp/llame-issue-327.md\\n+## Outcome\\n+\\n+Add a cheap, pointer-only `recent_chats` tool so the model can discover what the owner has worked on recently without inventing a semantic query or receiving historical message bodies automatically.\\n+\\n+Parent: #194\\n+\\n+## Result contract\\n+\\n+- Return a bounded recency-ordered list of chat identifiers, titles, last-activity timestamps, message counts, and pin state.\\n+- Return no message bodies, snippets, opening excerpts, summaries, embeddings, or semantic scores. This is an index into history, not history content.\\n+- Reuse the owner-scoped chat query path under `tenantDb.runAs`/RLS. Owner identity comes from the Run context and is not a model argument.\\n+- Exclude the current chat, archived chats, and untitled chats exactly as #307 does. Render pin state without duplicating a chat between pinned/recent groups.\\n+- Use a small server-clamped limit. No cursor until a measured caller needs pagination.\\n+- Return the same identifier shape consumed by #331; a transient requested result is the correct place to pay the identifier cost.\\n+\\n+## Consent and framing\\n+\\n+- Advertise only when #326's history-sharing master and #307's `shareRecentChats` setting are both enabled.\\n+- With either off, the declaration is absent from the immutable Run snapshot and receipt, and an emitted call is unexecutable.\\n+- Titles are untrusted owner-derived text. Preserve the same reserved-tag/framing protections as the digest.\\n+- The unauthenticated/shared-chat path has no owner identity and never receives the tool.\\n+\\n+## Acceptance criteria\\n+\\n+- [ ] The response contains only the declared pointer metadata; no historical content reaches it by any path.\\n+- [ ] A model-supplied identity field is impossible, and datastore-backed tests prove cross-owner/empty identity denial.\\n+- [ ] Master-off, recent-off, and both-off states each remove the declaration and execution path.\\n+- [ ] Master on + recent off keeps `search_conversations` while excluding `recent_chats`.\\n+- [ ] Exclusion and ordering rules match #307's digest for the same snapshot.\\n+- [ ] Instruction/tag-shaped titles cannot escape the result envelope.\\n+- [ ] The limit is schema-validated and server-clamped.\\n+\\n+## Boundaries\\n+\\n+No message content, semantic ranking, embeddings, search cursor, cross-owner reads, automatic tool invocation guarantee, or write capability. Recency orders pointers only and never affects retention/deletion.\\n+\\n+## Dependencies\\n+\\n+Requires #326's master gate; consumes the already-shipped #307 nested setting. Composes with #331 but does not block implementing its chat filter.\\n*** Add File: /tmp/llame-issue-331.md\\n+## Outcome\\n+\\n+Let `search_conversations` narrow to one owner-authorized chat so a compacted conversation can recover canonical details below its lossy checkpoint instead of treating the checkpoint as irreversible history.\\n+\\n+Parent: #194\\n+\\n+## Scope\\n+\\n+- Add one optional `chatId`/settled chat-identifier field. One chat is sufficient; multi-chat fan-out remains the unscoped search operation.\\n+- The filter only narrows. Owner identity still comes from the authenticated Run; FORCE RLS and the in-query owner predicate remain authoritative.\\n+- Reuse `searchByOwner`; do not create a parallel query path.\\n+- Preserve relevance ordering within the scoped search. Chronological expansion belongs to #609's bounded range read, not candidate ranking.\\n+- Teach the model the current chat identifier through trusted server-authored context at the compaction checkpoint. #327 supplies identifiers for other chats.\\n+- When absent, query behavior and ordering remain compatible.\\n+- Inherit #326's owner gate unchanged.\\n+\\n+## Acceptance criteria\\n+\\n+- [ ] No filter preserves existing results and ordering.\\n+- [ ] An owner-scoped filter returns candidates only from that chat.\\n+- [ ] Another owner's identifier, nonexistent identifier, malformed input, and empty identity fail as bounded structured observations; they never widen the search.\\n+- [ ] Datastore-backed tests prove cross-owner denial under FORCE RLS and the defense-in-depth owner predicate.\\n+- [ ] A server-authored compaction checkpoint supplies the current chat identifier and explains that superseded canonical