normalized text, embeddings, generated context, and translations are never represented as original evidence.\\n+- Do not expose a universal relevance/confidence float. Retrieval rank, current source validity, and answer support remain distinct.\\n+\\n+## Timeline mode\\n+\\n+- Qualify activity by canonical eligible message timestamps, not chat creation time or generic `updatedAt`.\\n+- Return one bounded activity region per qualifying chat containing only:\\n+  - `chatId` and title;\\n+  - `firstActivityAt` and `lastActivityAt` inside the requested range;\\n+  - the count of eligible messages inside the range;\\n+  - a server-issued #609 `sourceRef` for the canonical range.\\n+- Return no snippets, opening excerpts, message bodies, semantic scores, `matchedBy`, embeddings, summaries, or generated text. Without a content query there is no defensible excerpt-selection basis.\\n+- Use stable `lastActivityAt DESC` ordering and report `truncated` explicitly.\\n+- A listing request can stop at metadata. A recap or synthesis must follow the returned `sourceRef` values through bounded canonical reads.\\n+\\n+## Temporal interpretation\\n+\\n+- The tool accepts absolute ranges only; it does not parse natural-language dates.\\n+- The model resolves “yesterday”, “last week”, and similar phrases from trusted temporal context and the tool result echoes the normalized range actually used.\\n+- Exact phrases such as “yesterday” or “during March” use timeline mode or `required` content filtering.\\n+- Uncertain recollections such as “I think it was a few months ago” use `preferred` content search so an incorrect date memory cannot hide the right result.\\n+- “Recent” without a finite user-supplied period does not authorize an unbounded timeline scan. The model must materialize a finite range from its temporal context or ask the owner to clarify.\\n+- Until #454 ships, relative phrases use the instance timezone already exposed by #334/#408. #454 adds owner-local temporal context; it