al re-prioritization when the user's recollection may be wrong;\\n+- exact-source validity, speaker/time attribution, original-vs-generated-translation labeling, quote support, and multi-span support;\\n+- correctness given invocation, separately from invocation rate.\\n+\\n+Every returned quote must be exact current authorized canonical content. Citation exactness is a gate, not a score utility can trade against. Queryless timeline discovery returns pointers and coverage metadata rather than arbitrary excerpts; recap correctness is evaluated only after canonical reads.\\n+\\n+### 3. Governance and lifecycle\\n+\\n+Adapt GateMem's threat model as fail-closed gates:\\n+\\n+- cross-tenant and empty-identity retrieval;\\n+- delegated/socially engineered recovery attempts;\\n+- stale source after edit/deletion/permission change;\\n+- default-off and enabled states of `episodicMemory.enabled`;\\n+- disabling episodic memory after prior use, including the honest non-retroactive limits of already-bound prompts/history;\\n+- instruction-shaped historical content;\\n+- compaction recovery without bypassing corpus or authority boundaries.\\n+\\n+Any cross-tenant recall is P0 failure, never an averaged metric.\\n+\\n+## Benchmark use\\n+\\n+- **Primary external:** LongMemEval for temporal, multi-session, update, and abstention abilities.\\n+- **Invocation supplement:** context-driven/proactive retrieval benchmark, adapted rather than copied blindly.\\n+- **Governance gate:** GateMem threat model, not its aggregate score.\\n+- **Smoke only:** LoCoMo; an independent audit found material key/judge/category defects.\\n+- **Private llame corpus:** authoritative release signal for corpus routing, cross-language behavior, compaction, consent, citation, timeline coverage, and tenant isolation.\\n+\\n+## Reproducibility requirements\\n+\\n+- [ ] Version and pin answerer model, judge model, judge prompt, tool descriptions/schemas, dataset, category inclusion, retrieval depth, token budg